+We built a local, zero-cost, adaptive defense-in-depth pipeline for RAG and evaluated it stage by stage on 920 evaluation rows across seven sets, including independent PoisonedRAG, BIPIA, and deepset data, with paired tests within each set. The evidence supports four conclusions. \emph{Instruction-carrying attacks are strongly but not completely mitigated.} In Protocol~A no canary leaked into any delivered answer (0 of 132), and every poisoned chunk was redacted before generation (74/74). On delivered outputs, attack success falls from 29.5\% to 3.5\%, and 45 of 50 BIPIA attacks are blocked. Against existing defenses on the same attacks, the seven-layer stack clearly outperforms an LLM-Guard firewall, Spotlighting, and an adapted implementation of the ADO orchestrator and matches the ControlNET detector used as a firewall, with somewhat better benign utility. \emph{Three layers carry this defense against instruction-bearing attacks}, though not at lower latency (19.0 vs.\ 15.3\,s median). Sanitization, grounding, and refusal on detection act as near-independent filters; no replicate separates them from the seven-layer stack, and on an enlarged 424-row set the cost of dropping the other four layers is at most about 2.4 points (95\% bound), whereas no single layer comes close. On assertion-only attacks the subset is significantly worse, because the guarded prompt carries that defense. \emph{Conjunctive detection fails on saturated scores for a provable reason}, and a graded-margin repair restores it in-sample (24/30) but only modestly on external data (8/30). \emph{Signature-free knowledge corruption remains the dominant open risk}: 35\% of semantic-slice attacks still succeed; on PoisonedRAG the defended pipeline is no better than undefended RAG (90.7\% vs.\ 91.3\% ASR, $p=1.0$); and no answer-side signal we tested separates these attacks from benign traffic. The full stack runs on an 8\,GB laptop GPU at a median of 15.3\,s per query, or 8.6\,s with its encoders moved to the GPU in fp16.
+
+For practitioners: against instruction-bearing attacks, deploy per-chunk sanitization, grounding, and refusal on detection first, but keep the guarded prompt where planted factual claims matter; do not expect a co-activation detector to help unless its channels are graded; and treat knowledge corruption as an unmitigated risk that requires provenance controls or human review. More broadly, a defense-in-depth pipeline should be evaluated like any multi-component system: stage by stage, with claims traced to evidence, and with negative results reported as carefully as positive ones.
